{
  "schema_version": "1.7.0",
  "id": "USN-9065-1",
  "summary": "glibc vulnerabilities",
  "details": "It was discovered that the GNU C Library incorrectly handled the ccs=\noption in the fopen function. An attacker could possibly use this issue\nto cause GNU C Library to crash or execute arbitrary code.\n(CVE-2026-18374)\n\nIt was discovered that the GNU C Library incorrectly handled long domain\nnames in DNS search lists. An attacker on the local network could\npossibly use this issue to cause GNU C Library to crash, resulting in a\ndenial of service. (CVE-2026-8674)\n\nIt was discovered that the GNU C Library dynamic loader had a\ntime-of-check to time-of-use race condition when handling the $ORIGIN\npath expansion in setuid or setgid programs. A local attacker could\npossibly use this issue to execute arbitrary code with elevated\nprivileges. (CVE-2026-86805)\n\nIt was discovered that the GNU C Library dynamic loader incorrectly\nhandled $ORIGIN path expansion, leading to a stack-based buffer\noverflow. A local attacker could possibly use this issue to cause GNU C\nLibrary to crash, resulting in a denial of service, or obtain sensitive\ninformation. (CVE-2026-95818)\n\nIt was discovered that the GNU C Library nscd service incorrectly\nhandled large DNS responses. An attacker could possibly use this issue\nto cause nscd to crash, resulting in a denial of service.\n(CVE-2026-89092)\n\nIt was discovered that the GNU C Library strncasecmp function optimized\nfor the Power8 architecture incorrectly handled certain inputs. An\nattacker could possibly use this issue to cause GNU C Library to crash,\nresulting in a denial of service. (CVE-2026-97399)\n\nIt was discovered that the GNU C Library incorrectly restricted path\ntraversal when processing the LANGUAGE environment variable in gettext.\nA local attacker could possibly use this issue to load message catalogs\nfrom arbitrary locations and execute arbitrary code.\n(CVE-2026-84243)",
